Leaf verification rules:
- Check every emitted field against its schema description and the page
  evidence. Use the page image to align labels and value columns, and use
  the prepared page text above to transcribe exact text.
- For every value, transcribe the source first and then apply only the
  transformations required by that field's description. Preserve every source
  character or delimiter that the description does not explicitly remove or
  reformat. Never borrow a transformation from another field.
- Re-enumerate every visual row with a value in at least one data column named
  by the selected array schema. Each must appear exactly once. A bold, indented,
  or subgroup label with data is still the subject of a data row.
- Reconcile that enumeration against the supplied page-table row inventory.
  Every inventoried source line with a value in a schema data column must map to
  one emitted row. Ignore inventoried headers, section-only rows, separators,
  and blanks rather than treating every inventoried line as data.
- For a field defined as the nearest preceding section-only or group heading, a
  line qualifies only when its visual row has no values in any data column.
  Assign headings only after all data rows have been enumerated. A data-bearing
  label must retain the preceding qualifying heading and must not replace it.
- Apply the active qualifying heading to every following data row until another
  qualifying heading appears. When a field description names a heading category,
  use only the printed line or block that satisfies that category. Do not append
  an adjacent title, subtitle, description, or document label.
- Re-audit every string-valued cell against its field description. Removing
  whitespace inside a source delimiter never removes the delimiter itself.
  Preserve all source delimiters unless that field description explicitly says
  to remove them. Whitespace-only normalization inside a delimiter pair changes
  `( value )` to `(value)`, never to `value`.
- Re-audit every carried heading field. If its value is copied from a row that
  has any data-column value, it is not a qualifying heading; restore the nearest
  preceding value-less heading, or emit null on a continuation page so the
  ordered parser can carry it.
- For a category-qualified heading, stop before an adjacent line whose role is
  a title, subtitle, content-type label, or description rather than part of that
  category heading.
- Recheck the selected schema unit and every continuation decision. A plausible
  value in the wrong unit is still incorrect.

Continuation rules:
- Audit the supplied continuation flag against any current-of-total marker in
  the page text. For a marker belonging to the table, current 1 means a new
  table page and current greater than 1 means a continuation. Use the marker
  when the supplied flag contradicts it.
- A table marked as a continuation after that audit is not a new schema unit,
  whether or not headers repeat.
- A continuation whose owning unit is not explicitly identifiable on this page
  must have unit_name null. Do not classify it by subject matter.
- Leave an aggregation-policy carry field null when its value is omitted from a
  continuation page.
- Keep aggregation-policy sequence fields page-local and 1-based. The parser
  assigns the document-wide sequence.
- Do not merge records or emit rows from previous pages. The ordered parser
  performs cross-page aggregation after this pass.

